# Prettier's ignore scope for the whole workspace. Every `check` script points here with
# --ignore-path, so build output and generated code are excluded once, at the root, instead of
# once per app. Patterns are gitignore syntax; the ones without a slash match at any depth, so
# they hold whichever directory prettier runs from.

# Dependencies and package-manager state
node_modules/
pnpm-lock.yaml

# Build output — `build` rewrites it, so any formatting applied here is undone by the next run
out/
dist/
.output/
.vite/
.tanstack/
.turbo/
coverage/

# Generated code — owned by its generator, which rewrites it wholesale
routeTree.gen.ts

# Prose that prettier does not own.
#
# No app in this workspace has a `check` script since Maestro (the one that did) moved to its own
# repository — these directories were never actually checked, but nothing said so, and running
# prettier from the root reported ~110 failures that read as rot rather than as scope. They are
# documentation, task files and SKILL.md bodies: hand-formatted, read as much in a GitHub blob and
# a Claude context window as in an editor, and reflowing them would rewrite line breaks that were
# chosen. Stated here so the answer is a decision on the record instead of a surprise for whoever
# next runs prettier from the root.
#
# EVERY PATTERN IS ROOT-ANCHORED, and that is load-bearing rather than tidy — a bare `skills/`
# would match at any depth instead of holding just the repo-root directory of that name.
/docs/
/skills/
/rules/
/agents/
/incubator/
/plugins/
/.claude/

# NOT ignored, and deliberately: `packages/` and the root's own README/CHANGELOG/TODO. Those are
# maintained files, not prose prettier has no business in — even though, with Maestro's `check`
# script gone along with it, nothing in this workspace currently runs `prettier --check` against
# them either. Closing that gap is a `check` script on some future package, not a line here.
